  "nameStory": "The aborted entoloma gets its quirky name from its unusual, misshapen appearance that looks nothing like a typical mushroom. It's also called 'shrimp of the woods' because these irregular, lumpy masses often resemble cooked shellfish. The 'aborted' part refers to how the mushroom's normal development gets hijacked when it parasitizes honey mushrooms, creating these weird, wonderful forms.",
  "description": "The aborted entoloma is one of nature's shape-shifters, appearing in two dramatically different forms. Sometimes it looks like a normal grayish mushroom with a cap up to 3 inches wide and pinkish gills, but more often you'll find it as irregular, lumpy masses that look like cauliflower or cooked shrimp. These odd formations happen when the fungus parasitizes honey mushrooms, creating the bizarre 'aborted' shapes that make this species so distinctive. The whole fungus can grow quite large, with stems reaching up to 9 cm long when in normal mushroom form.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Shape",
      "description": "Look for irregular, lumpy white to pinkish masses that resemble cauliflower, popcorn, or cooked shrimp - these are the distinctive 'aborted' forms that make this species unmistakable",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "When in normal mushroom form, the cap is grayish and can reach 3 inches across with a smooth to slightly textured surface",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "Normal forms have attached gills that start white and turn pinkish as spores mature, but the aborted masses lack gills entirely",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smell",
      "description": "Has a distinctive mealy or flour-like odor that's quite noticeable when you get close",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "Produces a pinkish spore print from normal mushroom forms, though the aborted masses rarely produce spores",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Bruising",
      "description": "The white to pinkish flesh of aborted forms feels firm and doesn't change color significantly when cut or bruised",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found growing on or near decaying hardwood logs, stumps, and buried wood in mixed and deciduous forests throughout New England. Often appears to be growing from soil but is actually connected to buried wood or root systems. Look for it in areas where honey mushrooms also grow, since this species parasitizes them.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Rarely found in spring, though some early specimens may appear in late May during warm, wet periods.",
    "summer": "Begins appearing in mid to late summer, especially after periods of rain and humidity. Both normal and aborted forms can be found.",
    "fall": "Peak season runs from late August through October, when the most abundant and largest specimens appear. This is the best time to find the characteristic aborted forms.",
    "winter": "Disappears with hard frosts, though dried specimens may persist on logs through early winter."
  },
  "funFact": "Scientists once thought honey mushrooms were parasitizing this entoloma to create the weird shapes, but it's actually the opposite - the entoloma is the parasite! It hijacks the honey mushroom's growth, turning it into those bizarre lumpy masses that look nothing like either original fungus.",
  "confusionNotes": "The aborted forms are so distinctive they're hard to confuse with anything else, but normal mushroom forms could be mistaken for the poisonous livid pinkgill (Entoloma sinuatum). The livid pinkgill is much larger, has a yellowish tint, and lacks the mealy smell. Memory trick: if it smells like flour and you see lumpy masses nearby, you've found the right species.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "This species has had quite the taxonomic journey, originally being placed in the genus Clitopilus before finding its current home in Entoloma. The parasitic relationship with honey mushrooms wasn't understood until relatively recently, showing how much we're still learning about fungal interactions in the forest.",
  "compareHints": "Unlike other pinkgills in the Entoloma genus, aborted entoloma is the only one that regularly produces those characteristic lumpy, aborted forms. Most other entolomas maintain their typical mushroom shape and grow directly from soil or wood. The parasitic lifestyle and resulting shape-shifting ability make this species unique among New England's pinkgills.",
